 Zoey Blackman, Trevor Higgins.0 aFeaturing: Wayne Heideman, Scott Bates.  a"Beavers can get a bad rap. Some people consider them pests because they dam up waterways and take up a lot of space. But beaver activity creates habitat for a diversity of plants and animals and helps improve water quality. So it's worth it to work with beavers and coexist. Beaver are ecosystems engineers that have a positive ripple effect on our environment"--NPS video description.1 aClosed captioning available in English1 aAudio description available in English  aDescription based on online resource; title from landing page (NPS, viewed June 26, 2026). 0aHuman-animal relationships. 0aBeaversxEcologyzUnited States. 7aInternet videos.2lcgft 7aVideo recordings.2lcgft1 aKauffman, Ethan,edirector.1 aMinges, Madison,edirector.1 aBabinski, Katie,edirector.1 aUnited States.bNational Park Service,eissuing body.40uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo257575704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.nps.gov/media/video/view.htm?id=BE7473AA-C982-4DEF-8ED8-2E2C97C917F5  aC0bGPO  abc108 20260708b20260708  aUNREPORTEDPUBSSTAFFb2026070803497ngm a2200661 i 00147599220260708143246.0m     o  c f      cr cna||||||||vz czazz|260708e20251001dcu007       fo   vleng d  a(OCoLC)1602648694  aGPObengerdaepncGPO0 aengpengqeng  an-us-mdan-us-va  aXZL4  a0648 (online)0 aI 29.2:F 85/60 aFrankenfish (Motion picture)10aFrankenfish. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bNational Park Service,c2025.  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (6 min., 50 sec.)) :bsound, color  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aauditorybclosedcaptions2sapdv0 avisualdaudioDescription2sapdv  avideo file2rdaft  a"10/01/2025"--NPS website.  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aProducer, Krystal Shaw ; editor, Abby Cannon ; sound recordist, Jonathon Woolford-Hunt ; writer, Krystal Shaw ; interviewer, Abby Cannon ; composer, Jonathon Woolford-Hunt ; Go-Pro operator, Jonathon Woolford-Hunt ; camera operators, Jonathon Woolford-Hunt, Abby Cannon, and Krystal Shaw.0 aFeaturing: Branson Williams, Chris Adriance, Eric Thadey, Joe Swann, David Rudow.  aFilmed on location at Rock Creek Park, Kenilworth Park & Aquatic Gardens, Maryland Department of Natural Resources, and along the Chesapeake bay.  a"Northern snakehead is probably the best known of the "Big 3" invasive fish in the Potomac River and Chesapeake Bay. Along with blue catfish and flathead catfish, snakehead are opportunistic predators that eat and compete with native fish species. Learn more about this "Frankenfish" and what you can do to help"--NPS website.1 aClosed captioning available in English1 aAudio description available in English2 aAudio description could not be accessed or evaluated due to a media loading error  aDescription based on online resource; title from landing page (NPS, viewed May 22, 2026). 0aIctalurus furcatus. 0aNorthern snakeheadzPotomac River. 0aNorthern snakeheadzChesapeake Bay (Md. and Va.) 0aFlathead catfishzPotomac River. 0aFlathead catfishzChesapeake Bay (Md. and Va.) 0aIntroduced freshwater organismszPotomac River. 0aIntroduced freshwater organismszChesapeake Bay (Md. and Va.) 7aInternet videos.2lcgft 7aVideo recordings.2lcgft1 aCannon, Abby,edirector.1 aShaw, Krystal,edirector.1 aWoolford-Hunt, Jonathon,edirector.1 aUnited States.bNational Park Service,eissuing body.40uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo256371704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://www.nps.gov/media/video/view.htm?id=46658237-F274-4F18-92C2-161F75F3682C  aC0bGPO  abc108 20260708b20260708  aUNREPORTEDPUBSSTAFFb2026070802810ngm a2200565 i 00147599320260708143559.0m     o  c f      cr cna||||||||vz czazz|260708e20251001dcu006       fo   vleng d  a(OCoLC)1602648753  aGPObengerdaepncGPO0 aengpengqeng  an-us---  aXZL4  a0648 (online)0 aI 29.2:T 86/30 aTurtles in trouble (Motion picture)10aTurtles in trouble. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bNational Park Service,c2025.  a1 online resource (1 streaming video file (5 min., 56 sec.)) :bsound, color  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aauditorybclosedCaptions2sapdv0 avisualdaudioDescription2sapdv  avideo file2rdaft  a"10/01/2025"--NPS website.  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  a"With recent declines in turtle populations, the National Park Service is working with scientists to study how to help protect them. Turtles are long lived, slow to mature, and take a long time to be able to reproduce, so every individual in a population is important to protect. We can help turtles by safeguarding clean water and wetlands and learning more about these amazing creatures"--NPS video description.1 aClosed captioning available in English1 aAudio description available in English  aDescription based on online resource; title from landing page (NPS, viewed June 26, 2026). 0aTurtlesxConservationzUnited States. 0aWetland conservationzUnited States. 7aInternet videos.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026337 7aVideo recordings.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110267231 aNold, Lily,edirector.1 aJelle, Chris,edirector.1 aChesleigh, Kaitlyn,edirector.1 aUnited States.bNational Park Service,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.go