tion capabilities :blarge and small island operations /cArmy University Films presents ; in collaboration with the Joint Center for International Security Force Assistance and the Army Transformation and Training Command G2. 1a[Fort Leavenworth, Kan.] :bArmy University Press,c2026.  a1 online resource (1 video file (48 min., 37 sec.)) :bsound, color  atwo-dimensional moving imagebtdi2rdacontent  avideobv2rdamedia  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aauditorybclosedCaptions2sapdv  adigital2rda  avideo file2rdaft  a"06.29.2026"--DVIDS website.  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aDirector and producer, Randy G. Masten ; assistant director, Peter Wood ; writer, Peter Wood ; contributing writer, Randy G. Masten ; executive producers, COL Andrew Steadman, Don Wright ; video, Kyle Davis.  a"This Army documentary provides an overview of the United States Pacific Command (USPACOM). It examines China's current worldview, and its rapid rise in force projection capabilities. The film looks at the People's Liberation Army (PLA), Chinese military history, and breaks down the size and scope of the PLA's forces. China's military doctrine is discussed including small and large-island seizure operations, as well as the phases of amphibious landing operations"--DVIDS website.1 aClosed captioning available in English  aDescription based on online resource; title from landing page (dvidshub.net, viewed July 31, 2026)10aUnited States.bPacific Command.10aChina.bZhongguo ren min jie fang jun. 0aAmphibious warfare. 0aDeployment (Strategy) 0aPacific AreaxStrategic aspects. 0aMilitary art and sciencezChina. 7aDocumentary films.2lcgft 7aNonfiction films.2lcgft 7aInternet videos.2lcgft1 aMasten, Ran